var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"#AnnotatedTests.jl","page":"Home","text":"AnnotatedTests.jl adds named, annotated tests to Julia's standard testing workflow. It is intended for teaching contexts where a failing test should give a student a concrete next step, not only a Boolean failure.\n\n@annotated_test \"absolute value\" abs(x) >= 0 ctx -> \"The absolute value should never be negative.\"","title":"AnnotatedTests.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-Start","page":"Home","text":"using AnnotatedTests\n\n@annotated_test \"sorts a vector\" mysort([3, 1, 2]) == [1, 2, 3] \\\n    \"Return the values in increasing order.\"\n\n@atest \"same values\" sort(student_values()) == [1, 2, 3] unordered_feedback()\n\nUse @annotated_testset when you want a pedagogical name for a group of related checks:\n\n@annotated_testset \"Assignment 1\" begin\n    @annotated_test \"question 1\" q1() == 42 \"Review the formula for q1.\"\nend\n\nThe feedback handler may be a string, a function, or a callable object. Function handlers receive an AnnotationContext, which includes evaluated left and right values for supported binary comparisons. The traditional ctx.lhs and ctx.rhs names are available alongside teaching-oriented aliases: ctx.observed, ctx.expected, ctx.LHS, and ctx.RHS.","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Supported-Comparisons","page":"Home","text":"AnnotatedTests captures the left and right side of ==, !=, <, <=, >, >=, ===, !==, in, ∈, isa, and ≈. Each side is evaluated once.\n\nFor non-binary expressions, the context still records the test name and original expression, but the binary-expression fields are nothing.\n\n≈ also records ctx.difference when a difference can be computed.","title":"Supported Comparisons"},{"category":"section","location":"#Feedback-Helpers","page":"Home","text":"@atest \"close answer\" estimate() ≈ 10 expected_feedback(\"Check your rounding.\")\n@atest \"right type\" answer() isa Vector{Int} type_feedback()\n@atest \"right length\" answer() == expected length_feedback()\n@atest \"same items\" answer() == expected unordered_feedback()","title":"Feedback Helpers"},{"category":"section","location":"#Broken-Tests","page":"Home","text":"@annotated_broken \"stretch goal\" advanced_answer() == expected \\\n    \"This check documents the next feature to implement.\"","title":"Broken Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"#Custom-Operators","page":"Home","text":"Use register_annotated_operator! to add a relation and optional terms:\n\nwithin10(x, y) = abs(x - y) <= 10\n\nregister_annotated_operator!(:within10; terms=(lhs, rhs) -> (\n    difference = abs(lhs - rhs),\n))\n\n@atest \"within tolerance\" within10(student_answer(), 100) ctx ->\n    \"The difference was $(ctx.difference); it must be at most 10.\"","title":"Custom Operators"},{"category":"section","location":"#API","page":"Home","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"macro","location":"#AnnotatedTests.@annotated_test","page":"Home","text":"@annotated_test name expr [on_error]\n@atest name expr [on_error]\n\nRun a test like Test.@test, but attach teacher-written feedback when it fails. The feedback handler may be a string, function, or callable object. Functions receive an AnnotationContext.\n\nThe macro evaluates each side of a supported binary comparison exactly once, so expressions with side effects such as pop!(xs) == 3 are safe to annotate.\n\nExamples\n\n@annotated_test \"sorted output\" mysort([3, 1, 2]) == [1, 2, 3] \\\n    \"Return the values in increasing order.\"\n\n@atest \"close enough\" estimate() ≈ 10 ctx ->\n    \"Difference was $(ctx.difference).\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.@annotated_test"},{"category":"macro","location":"#AnnotatedTests.@atest","page":"Home","text":"@atest name expr [on_error]\n\nShort alias for @annotated_test.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.@atest"},{"category":"macro","location":"#AnnotatedTests.@annotated_broken","page":"Home","text":"@annotated_broken name expr [on_error]\n\nRecord an annotated test as broken. This mirrors Test.@test_broken: a false condition is recorded as a broken test, while an unexpectedly true condition is reported by the test framework.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.@annotated_broken"},{"category":"macro","location":"#AnnotatedTests.@annotated_testset","page":"Home","text":"@annotated_testset name begin ... end\n\nA light wrapper over Test.@testset for pedagogical test groups.\n\nExample\n\n@annotated_testset \"Assignment 1\" begin\n    @annotated_test \"question 1\" q1() == 42 \"Review the formula for q1.\"\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.@annotated_testset"},{"category":"type","location":"#AnnotatedTests.AnnotationContext","page":"Home","text":"AnnotationContext\n\nStructured information made available to feedback handlers when an annotated test fails.\n\nFields include the test name, the original test expression expr, the detected binary operator op when there is one, the unevaluated left and right expressions, their evaluated values, the final Boolean value, and any operator-specific terms.\n\nFor non-binary expressions, op, lhs_expr, rhs_expr, lhs, and rhs are nothing.\n\nAliases are available for common teaching language:\n\nctx.observed and ctx.LHS are aliases for ctx.lhs\nctx.expected and ctx.RHS are aliases for ctx.rhs\noperator-specific terms such as ctx.difference are read from ctx.terms\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.AnnotationContext"},{"category":"function","location":"#AnnotatedTests.default_feedback","page":"Home","text":"default_feedback(ctx::AnnotationContext)\n\nDefault human-readable feedback message for a failed annotated test.\n\nThis function is used when @annotated_test is called without an explicit feedback handler. It reports the test name, expression, observed and expected values for supported binary comparisons, and operator-specific terms such as difference when available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.default_feedback"},{"category":"function","location":"#AnnotatedTests.compare_feedback","page":"Home","text":"compare_feedback(; message=nothing, observed_label=\"Observed\", expected_label=\"Expected\")\n\nCreate a feedback handler for ordinary comparisons. The returned handler prints the original expression, observed value, expected value, and any operator terms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.compare_feedback"},{"category":"function","location":"#AnnotatedTests.expected_feedback","page":"Home","text":"expected_feedback(message=\"Check the expected value.\"; kwargs...)\n\nCreate a short expected-vs-observed feedback handler. Keyword arguments are passed to compare_feedback.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.expected_feedback"},{"category":"function","location":"#AnnotatedTests.type_feedback","page":"Home","text":"type_feedback([expected])\n\nCreate feedback for type checks. If expected is omitted and the annotated expression uses isa, the right-hand side of the comparison is shown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.type_feedback"},{"category":"function","location":"#AnnotatedTests.length_feedback","page":"Home","text":"length_feedback([expected])\n\nCreate feedback that includes observed and expected lengths when they are available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.length_feedback"},{"category":"function","location":"#AnnotatedTests.unordered_feedback","page":"Home","text":"unordered_feedback()\n\nCreate feedback for collection comparisons where order might be the issue.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.unordered_feedback"},{"category":"function","location":"#AnnotatedTests.register_annotated_operator!","page":"Home","text":"register_annotated_operator!(op::Symbol; terms=(lhs, rhs) -> (;))\n\nRegister an operator or binary relation that @annotated_test should treat as a comparison. The optional terms function receives the evaluated left and right values and must return a NamedTuple.\n\nThose terms become available in feedback handlers as ctx.terms and as direct properties. For example, a term named difference can be read as ctx.difference.\n\nExample\n\nwithin10(x, y) = abs(x - y) <= 10\nregister_annotated_operator!(:within10; terms=(lhs, rhs) -> (difference=abs(lhs - rhs),))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AnnotatedTests.register_annotated_operator!"}]
}
